var e=1,t=`fsa-ebitda-01`,n=`financial-statement-analysis`,r=`EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA and What the Adjustments Cost You`,i=`The measure the whole leveraged finance industry runs on, why it exists, and how 15 of add-backs moves a purchase price by 75.`,a=`core`,o=[`fsa-statements-01`,`fsa-cashflow-01`],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`[[ebitda|EBITDA]] is earnings before interest, taxes, [[depreciation]] and amortisation. Each exclusion answers a specific question, and the measure is only defensible for the question it was built for.

**Before interest** — so two companies with different debt levels can be compared on operations.

**Before taxes** — so different tax positions and jurisdictions do not distort the comparison.

**Before depreciation and amortisation** — so a company with old written-down assets is comparable with one that has just rebuilt its plant.

What is left is an approximation of operating profitability independent of how the business is financed and how old its assets are. **That is a genuinely useful thing, and it is the reason EBITDA became the unit of account for leveraged finance:** a buyer proposing to change the [[capital-structure|capital structure]] entirely needs a measure that is neutral to the capital structure.

It is not a cash flow measure, it is not a profit measure, and it flatters capital-intensive businesses systematically — because the assets do need replacing, and depreciation is the accountant's estimate of that cost.`},{type:`concept`,id:`the-multiple-machine`,body:`**In private markets EBITDA is not just a metric, it is the denominator of almost everything.**

A purchase price is quoted as a multiple of EBITDA. Debt is sized as a multiple of EBITDA. [[covenant|Covenants]] are set as ratios to EBITDA. Management incentives are struck on EBITDA growth.

Which produces a structural consequence worth stating plainly: **every participant has an interest in a higher EBITDA figure, and the figure requires judgement.** The seller gets a higher price, the management team gets a lower hurdle, the lender gets a larger loan to book, and the buyer gets to justify the price it paid.

The arithmetic is unforgiving. At a 5x multiple, one unit of EBITDA is five units of enterprise value. A 15-unit adjustment moves the price by 75 — and moves reported leverage from 5.0x to 4.2x on identical debt.

**So the adjustments are not presentational. They are the negotiation, conducted in accounting language.**`},{type:`intuition`,id:`the-adjustment-ladder`,body:`Adjustments run from clearly legitimate to clearly aspirational, and it is a spectrum rather than a line. Ordered from most to least defensible:

**Owner compensation normalisation.** A founder paying themselves 2m where the market rate for the role is 400k genuinely overstates the cost base for any new owner. Verifiable against market data. Legitimate.

**Genuine one-off costs.** A litigation settlement, a single restructuring, the fees of the transaction itself. Legitimate — provided "one-off" costs are not appearing every year, which they frequently do.

**Run-rate effects of completed actions.** A factory closed in September, with nine months of its costs still in the reported year. Defensible arithmetic, if the closure is complete and the savings are evidenced.

**Pro forma acquisitions.** Full-year effect of a business owned for four months. Defensible with real numbers, and it imports someone else's accounting.

**Run-rate synergies not yet delivered.** A forecast. Presented as history because it sits in the EBITDA line.

**Add-backs for costs that will recur.** "Exceptional" recruitment costs in a business with high turnover. Not an adjustment; a cost.

**The diagnostic that cuts through all of it: how many of last year's adjustments turned out to be real?** A seller who has bridged adjusted to actual for three prior years has evidence. One who has not is asking for trust, and the request is worth naming.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-adjustment`,body:`The worked company reports EBITDA of 80. The seller presents adjusted EBITDA of 95: +6 owner compensation normalisation, +4 one-off legal settlement, +5 run-rate synergies from a reorganisation announced last month. Debt is 400 and the price is being discussed at 5x.`,walkthrough:[`**Reported EBITDA 80.** Leverage = 400 / 80 = **5.0x**. Enterprise value at 5x = **400**.`,`**Adjusted EBITDA 95.** Leverage = 400 / 95 = **4.21x**. Enterprise value at 5x = **475**.`,`So the 15 of adjustments is worth **75 of price** and **0.79x of apparent leverage**, with nothing about the business changed.`,`Now grade them. The 6 of owner compensation is checkable against market pay data — take it. The 4 of legal settlement is legitimate if it is genuinely singular; check whether legal costs appeared in the prior three years.`,`The 5 of unrealised synergies is a forecast. If you accept it, you pay 25 today for savings that have not happened, and you take the delivery risk.`,`**Read that carefully: accepting 5 of unproven synergies at a 5x multiple means paying 25 of cash now for a plan.** Sellers are entitled to argue for it. Buyers are entitled to price it as a plan rather than as history.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **multiply every disputed adjustment by the multiple.** It converts an accounting argument into a cash number, which is the form in which it should reach an investment committee.`]},{type:`table`,id:`adjustment-table`,caption:`Grading an adjustment`,headers:[`Adjustment`,`Test that settles it`,`Usual verdict`],rows:[[`Owner compensation`,`Market rate for the role, from comparable data`,`Accept, at the market rate rather than the seller's figure.`],[`"One-off" costs`,`Did similar costs appear in each of the last three years?`,`Accept if genuinely singular; reject a recurring exception.`],[`Completed cost actions`,`Evidence the action is done and the saving is banked`,`Accept the run-rate of what is finished.`],[`Pro forma acquisition`,`Audited figures for the acquired business`,`Accept with verified numbers; adjust for accounting differences.`],[`Unrealised synergies`,`Nothing available — it has not happened`,`Price as a forecast, not as earnings. Or share the risk in the structure.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**EBITDA-based covenants with adjustment rights.** Loan documents increasingly permit borrower-defined add-backs, sometimes uncapped, sometimes including projected synergies. A covenant measured against a number the borrower can adjust is a covenant that does not bind — and this is documented rather than hidden, which means it is a reading problem rather than a discovery problem.

**Multiple arbitrage described as value creation.** Buying at 8x and selling at 10x produces a return that has nothing to do with the business. It is a real source of profit and it is not an operational achievement, and the distinction disappears in most attribution.

**Capital intensity ignored.** EBITDA treats a business needing 8% of revenue in annual capex identically to one needing 1%. Over a hold period the difference is enormous, and it is invisible at the EBITDA line.

**Comparing across accounting standards.** Lease treatment alone can shift EBITDA materially, because rent below the EBITDA line becomes depreciation and interest above it.

**Anchoring on the seller's number.** Once adjusted EBITDA becomes the base of every subsequent model, the argument has been conceded. Rebuild from the audited figure and rebuild your own bridge.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`leverage-on-adjusted`,body:`**A leverage multiple is only as meaningful as its denominator, and the denominator is usually adjusted.**

"Conservatively levered at 4.2x" and "levered at 5.0x" describe the identical loan on the identical company. The difference is which EBITDA sits underneath, and the adjusted figure is the one that appears in the marketing.

So any leverage figure needs one question attached: **4.2x of what?** Reported, adjusted, pro forma, or run-rate. The four can differ by more than a turn, and a turn of leverage is the difference between an investment-grade-adjacent structure and an aggressive one.

The practical discipline for a portfolio: **recompute leverage on reported EBITDA yourself and keep both numbers.** The gap between them is a measure of how much of the structure's comfort is accounting rather than cash — and it is also the first thing that closes when the business misses its plan, because the add-backs stop being credible at exactly the same moment.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask for the bridge from audited EBITDA to adjusted EBITDA, then multiply each disputed line by the transaction multiple.** It converts an accounting debate into the cash it actually represents.

**Ask how many of last year's adjustments were delivered.** A seller with a track record of accurate adjustments is a different counterparty from one without, and the question is easy to answer if the answer is good.

**Ask whether "one-off" costs appeared in each of the last three years.** Recurring exceptions are the most common and most casually accepted add-back.

**Read the covenant definition of EBITDA in the loan document, not the summary.** Uncapped borrower add-backs are common, disclosed, and rarely read.

**Compute leverage on reported EBITDA and keep both figures in the monitoring pack.** When performance slips, the adjusted figure loses credibility first, and you want the unadjusted number already on the page.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`EBITDA exists to compare operations independently of capital structure, tax and asset age — which is exactly what a buyer changing the capital structure needs, and why leveraged finance runs on it.`,`At a 5x multiple, 15 of adjustments is 75 of purchase price and 0.79x of apparent leverage, with nothing about the business changed.`,`Adjustments run from checkable (owner compensation at market rate) to aspirational (undelivered synergies). Grade each one and multiply by the multiple.`,`Always ask "leverage of what?" — reported, adjusted, pro forma and run-rate EBITDA can differ by more than a full turn of leverage.`]}],u=[{id:`fsa-ebitda-01-q1`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:2,tags:[`ebitda`,`leverage`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-adjustment`,stem:`Debt is 400 and adjusted EBITDA is 95. What is leverage, in turns? Give two decimal places.`,answer:4.21,tolerance:.05,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`x`,explanation:`On reported EBITDA of 80 the same debt is 5.00x. Nearly eight tenths of a turn of leverage came from the adjustment, which is the difference between how two committees would describe the identical loan.`},{id:`fsa-ebitda-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`ebitda`,`valuation`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-adjustment`,stem:`The seller's 15 of adjustments are accepted and the price is 5x EBITDA. By how much does the enterprise value rise?`,answer:75,tolerance:1,toleranceType:`abs`,explanation:`This is the number that should reach a committee — 75 of cash, not "15 of add-backs". The multiple converts every accounting argument into money, and it is the only form in which the argument can be weighed against anything else.`},{id:`fsa-ebitda-01-q3`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`ebitda`,`adjustments`],concept:`the-adjustment-ladder`,stem:`Which adjustment is least defensible?`,choices:[`Normalising an owner's salary to the market rate for the role`,`Adding back the run-rate of synergies from a reorganisation announced last month`,`Adding back the transaction's own advisory fees`,`Full-year pro forma effect of an audited business acquired in September`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Checkable against market pay data and genuinely changes for a new owner. The most defensible item on the list.`,`Correct. Nothing has happened yet. It is a forecast placed in an earnings line, and at a 5x multiple the buyer pays cash today for a plan whose delivery risk they also take.`,`Genuinely singular and directly caused by the deal itself. Standard and accepted.`,`Defensible with verified figures — it imports another company's accounting, which needs checking rather than rejecting.`],explanation:`The distinction that matters is evidence, not intent. Every item above the synergies line can be tested against something that already exists. Synergies can only be tested against the future, which is why the honest treatment is to structure for them — an earnout — rather than to pay for them.`},{id:`fsa-ebitda-01-q4`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:3,tags:[`ebitda`,`misconception`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`"EBITDA lets you compare any two companies' operating performance fairly." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — it neutralises capital structure, tax and asset age, but systematically flatters capital-intensive businesses whose assets genuinely need replacing`,`False — EBITDA cannot be compared between companies at all`,`True — that is precisely what it was designed for`,`False — because EBITDA is not defined by accounting standards`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Excluding depreciation removes a real economic cost. A business needing 8% of revenue in annual capex and one needing 1% look identical at the EBITDA line and are not remotely comparable.`,`Over-correction. Comparison is its purpose and it works well between businesses of similar capital intensity.`,`It was designed for that and only within a band of similar asset intensity — the qualification is the whole point.`,`True as a fact — EBITDA is not a defined GAAP measure — and not the reason the comparison fails here.`],explanation:`The practical fix is to look at EBITDA less capex, or at EBITDA margin alongside capex as a percentage of revenue. Two numbers instead of one, and it makes the capital intensity visible instead of assuming it away.`},{id:`fsa-ebitda-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`ebitda`,`covenants`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`A loan document defines covenant EBITDA to include borrower-determined add-backs for projected synergies, uncapped. What is the consequence?`,choices:[`The covenant is stricter than a standard definition`,`The covenant may not bind at all, since the borrower can raise the denominator by adding projections when performance deteriorates`,`The interest rate must be higher to compensate`,`The lender has no recourse in a default`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The opposite: a wider definition of EBITDA makes every ratio look better and the covenant looser.`,`Correct. A ratio test whose denominator the borrower can adjust is a test the borrower can pass. Uncapped synergy add-backs mean the covenant is most easily satisfied precisely when the business is missing its plan.`,`Pricing may or may not reflect it, and it does not restore the protection.`,`Recourse exists on payment default — the issue is that the covenant may never trip to give early warning.`],explanation:`This is documented rather than hidden, which makes it a reading problem. The question worth asking of any credit fund: what proportion of your portfolio has uncapped EBITDA add-backs in the covenant definition? A manager who tracks it has read the documents; one who does not has read the summaries.`},{id:`fsa-ebitda-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`ebitda`,`attribution`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`A buyout fund reports a 2.5x return on a deal where EBITDA grew 20%, debt was repaid, and the exit multiple was 10x against an entry of 8x. What deserves the most scrutiny in the attribution?`,choices:[`The EBITDA growth, since 20% is implausible`,`The multiple expansion from 8x to 10x, which is market repricing rather than anything the manager did`,`The debt repayment, which is not a source of return`,`Nothing — the components are all legitimate`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`20% EBITDA growth over a hold period is entirely ordinary and is the part most attributable to the manager.`,`Correct. Multiple expansion is real money and it is not an operational achievement. It reflects what the market was willing to pay, and it does not repeat on demand — so it should be separated in any claim of repeatable skill.`,`Debt repayment does contribute to equity return: less debt at exit means more of the enterprise value belongs to the equity.`,`They are legitimate as sources of profit. The scrutiny is about which ones are evidence of skill.`],explanation:`Ask for attribution split into EBITDA growth, multiple change and leverage. A manager whose returns come mostly from multiple expansion has been well positioned rather than skilful — worth knowing before assuming the record repeats in a market where multiples are not rising.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
//# sourceMappingURL=fsa-ebitda-01-ySAMnHbm.js.map